---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';
import wineries from '../../data/wineries.json';

const dePortola = [...wineries]
  .filter((w) => String((w as any).corridor || '').toLowerCase().includes('de portola'))
  .sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0));

const nearby = [...wineries]
  .filter((w) => {
    const c = String((w as any).corridor || '').toLowerCase();
    return c.includes('calle contento') || c.includes('de portola');
  })
  .sort((a, b) => (b.rating || 0) - (a.rating || 0))
  .slice(0, 10);

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'De Portola Road Wine Tasting Guide — Temecula',
  datePublished: '2026-09-06',
  dateModified: '2026-09-06',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bellie Acres Wine' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bellie Acres Wine', url: 'https://bellieacreswine.com/' },
  description:
    'De Portola Road Temecula wine tasting guide — why this corridor feels different, how to cluster stops, who it suits, and practical pacing tips.',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://bellieacreswine.com/guides/de-portola-road-wine-tasting/',
};
---

<Layout
  title="De Portola Road Wine Tasting Guide | Temecula"
  description="De Portola Road wine tasting in Temecula: corridor character, boutique energy, how to plan 2–3 stops, drive logic, and linked estates from our directory."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Navbar />
  <main class="bg-[#faf7f2]">
    <header class="border-b border-stone-200 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
        <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[2px] text-[var(--accent)] font-medium mb-3">Guide · Corridor · Sep 2026</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-serif tracking-tight text-stone-900 leading-[1.1]">
          De Portola Road Wine Tasting Guide
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg sm:text-xl text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
          De Portola is where many visitors go when they want Temecula’s hills and vineyard views without living all day
          on the busiest main-road energy. This guide explains the corridor’s character, who it fits, how to cluster
          tastings, and how to pair it with a calmer day plan.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-stone-500">
          Independent guide from <a href="/about" class="underline hover:text-stone-800">Bellie Acres Wine</a>. Hours,
          fees, and pet policies change — confirm with each winery. 21+ for tastings.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-12 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">What De Portola feels like</h2>
        <p>
          Temecula Valley is not one street. Rancho California Road carries a lot of the destination-resort gravity —
          larger properties, heavy weekend flow, easy discovery for first-timers who type “Temecula wineries” and follow
          the biggest names. <strong>De Portola Road</strong> sits in a different emotional register: more rolling
          agricultural scenery, more boutique and family-estate energy, and a pacing that rewards people who like the
          drive as part of the tasting.
        </p>
        <p>
          That does not mean “empty” or “always quiet.” Popular De Portola addresses still book out on peak Saturdays,
          especially in harvest season. It means the corridor’s default story is vineyard edges, smaller parking stories,
          and flights where you can still hear the person across the table. If your group wants nightclub volume, this
          may not be your only day. If your group wants landscape and conversation, start here.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use the <a href="/map" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline font-medium">map</a> to see how De Portola relates
          to Calle Contento and the main Rancho spine. Clustering is the whole game — a beautiful corridor still becomes
          exhausting if you bounce to the opposite side of the valley between every pour.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-3xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-serif text-stone-900">Who this corridor is for</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Couples and small groups who want views and a slightly softer social volume</li>
          <li>Return visitors who already did the big-name main-road circuit</li>
          <li>Photographers and “sip with a view” travelers (pair with our scenic guide)</li>
          <li>People building a two-day trip: one day Rancho-heavy, one day De Portola-heavy</li>
          <li>Designated-driver-friendly itineraries with shorter hops between stops</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="pt-2">
          Who might prefer a different anchor day: first-timers who want maximum amenities and on-site restaurants under
          one roof; large party-bus energy; guests who need the most frequent ride-hail density at the busiest nodes.
          Even then, a single De Portola stop can be the palate cleanser in a mixed day.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">How many stops on De Portola?</h2>
        <p>
          Match the corridor to the stop-count logic in our
          <a href="/guides/how-many-wineries-one-day" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">how many wineries in one day</a>
          guide. On De Portola specifically:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Two stops:</strong> Excellent for heat days, deep conversation, or a long lunch elsewhere.</li>
          <li><strong>Three stops:</strong> The sweet spot when you stay on/near the corridor all day.</li>
          <li><strong>Four stops:</strong> Only with spit discipline, early start, and almost no cross-valley detours.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Boutique flights can run long because hosts like to talk — budget time generously. That is a feature, not a
          bug, if you came for hospitality rather than a checklist.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Building a De Portola day</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Morning:</strong> Start earlier than you think, especially on weekends. Morning light on the vines is
          part of the product, and check-in lines are shorter. Bring water in the car before you taste anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Midday:</strong> Protect food. Some estates have robust kitchens; others are tasting-focused. If lunch
          is uncertain, plan a deliberate break in Old Town Temecula or a known restaurant rather than gambling on “we’ll
          figure it out between pours.” Palate and mood both depend on calories.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Afternoon:</strong> Heat climbs. Choose shaded patios, indoor options, or shorter final flights. If
          harvest events are on, verify whether your “quiet boutique” stop is actually hosting a stomp or ticketed party
          that day — read
          <a href="/guides/temecula-harvest-season" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">harvest season</a>
          and estate event pages.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Reservations:</strong> Weekends still want holds. Weekdays are more forgiving but not a void —
          special experiences and small seatings fill. See
          <a href="/guides/weekday-vs-weekend-tasting" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">weekday vs weekend</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Estates tagged De Portola in our directory</h2>
        <p>
          Listings below reflect corridor tags in our data. Always confirm location and access on the winery’s official
          site — roads, gates, and tasting venues evolve.
        </p>
        {dePortola.length === 0 ? (
          <p>
            Corridor tags may be incomplete for some estates — browse the full
            <a href="/wineries" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">wineries list</a> and filter geographically on the
            <a href="/map" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">map</a>.
          </p>
        ) : (
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            {dePortola.map((w) => (
              <li class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white px-4 py-3">
                <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`} class="font-medium text-stone-900 hover:text-[var(--accent)]">
                  {w.name}
                </a>
                <span class="text-stone-500 text-sm">
                  {' '}
                  · {w.price_range} · {w.rating}/5
                  {w.pet_friendly ? ' · pet-friendly tag' : ''}
                  {w.has_views ? ' · views tag' : ''}
                </span>
                {w.description && <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mt-1 line-clamp-2">{w.description}</p>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )}
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Nearby corridor companions</h2>
        <p>
          De Portola days often share logic with adjacent quieter pockets (including Calle Contento tags in our data).
          Sample highly rated nearby/corridor-adjacent picks to mix carefully without sprinting back to the heaviest
          main-road congestion between every glass:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          {nearby.map((w) => (
            <li class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white px-4 py-3 text-sm">
              <a href={`/wineries/${w.slug}`} class="font-medium text-stone-900 hover:text-[var(--accent)]">
                {w.name}
              </a>
              <span class="text-stone-500"> · {(w as any).corridor || w.location}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Views, pets, and practical packing</h2>
        <p>
          Many De Portola experiences are outdoor-forward. That is wonderful until wind, sun, or a surprised dog meets a
          tasting flight. Skim
          <a href="/guides/sip-with-a-view" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">Sip with a View</a>
          and
          <a href="/guides/pet-friendly-wineries" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">pet-friendly wineries</a>
          before you promise a patio to someone in heels or a reactive pup.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Closed-toe shoes help on gravel and vineyard-edge paths.</li>
          <li>Layers beat a single fashion choice — mornings cool, afternoons bright.</li>
          <li>Sunscreen, hat, refillable water, and a simple snack in the car.</li>
          <li>Cashless is common; still keep a card backup and rideshare app logged in.</li>
          <li>Spit cup comfort: if you are driving later, behave like a professional taster.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">De Portola vs Rancho California Road</h2>
        <p>
          Think of Rancho as the high-discovery boulevard: big signs, big calendars, big group capacity. Think of De
          Portola as the scenic working-vineyard lane: still professional hospitality, often more intimate scale. Neither
          is “better.” They solve different days.
        </p>
        <p>
          A strong two-day Temecula trip is one day weighted toward destination amenities and one day weighted toward
          corridor scenery — not six random pins connected by hope. Download the free
          <a href="/wine-day-planner/" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">Wine Day Planner</a>
          and assign De Portola to Day 1 or Day 2 deliberately.
        </p>
        <p>
          First visit only have one day? You can still do a hybrid: one main-road landmark stop plus two De Portola
          tastings — if and only if the drive order is clean on the map. Order of operations beats brand-name collecting.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Wine styles you may meet</h2>
        <p>
          Temecula’s strength is range. On and near De Portola you will still see Bordeaux-leaning reds, Rhône
          inspirations, Italian-leaning grapes, crisp whites, and occasional sparkling or dessert pours depending on the
          estate. Do not assume “quiet road = only light wine.” Read
          <a href="/guides/temecula-red-varietals" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">Temecula red varietals</a>
          if you want a style primer before you buy club memberships in a moment of patio joy.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ask what was picked recently in harvest months. Staff answers change weekly — that live detail is half the fun
          of visiting during crush season.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Responsible tasting on a scenic road</h2>
        <p>
          Scenic corridors are still real roads with real enforcement and real families sharing the lane. Plan a
          designated driver, a tour operator, or rideshare segments. Spit more than you swallow when you are moving
          between estates. If anyone in the car is “surprised” by how they feel after stop two, cancel stop three. The
          vines will be there next season.
        </p>
        <p>
          We are an independent publisher, not a winery and not a transportation company. We do not sell alcohol on this
          site. Our job is to help you plan a day you will remember for the right reasons.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-stone-900">Bottom line</h2>
        <p>
          De Portola Road is one of Temecula’s best answers to “we want wine country, not only a busy boulevard.” Cluster
          two or three stops, feed people, respect heat and harvest calendars, and use the map before you book. For
          contrast and amenities, keep Rancho California in your toolkit — just do not make every pin a mandatory trophy
          in a single afternoon.
        </p>
        <p>
          Continue with
          <a href="/guides/how-many-wineries-one-day" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">how many wineries per day</a>,
          <a href="/guides/best-temecula-wineries" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">best Temecula wineries</a>,
          or the full
          <a href="/guides" class="text-[var(--accent)] underline">guides library</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
